<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galich, Kostroma Oblast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Galich Mersky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galich is a small town on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-galich">Lake Galich</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, first mentioned in the 12th century. Known for its salt springs, it was the seat of a principality from the 13th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> annexed it in 1363. In the 15th century its princes, Yuri Dmitrievich and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a>, fought Vasily II for the Moscow throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galich-kostroma-oblast.jpg" alt="Galich, Kostroma Oblast" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olga Shuklina · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galich_starotorzh_svyat_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
